         "id": "SECTION_local_content",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgUniverseTimeInflation\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgUniverseTimeInflation', '0%')\" src=\"\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: The universe across space and time, from the Big Bang to today (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a1a7c478-9061-4160-ba43-301c081733e9",
                     "text": "©ESA",
                     "page": "The Universe Through Space-Time",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Cosmic Inflation\n\nThe picture above shows the history of the universe — from the Big Bang to today. Scientists describe it with a model called Lambda-CDM. Right after the Big Bang, the universe began to grow bigger and bigger, extremely fast. In the picture, that burst forms a kind of cone. This short period is called **Cosmic Inflation** (<htcite0>).\n\n#### What was the beginning like?\n\nThanks to Edwin Hubble, we know that the universe is expanding (<htcite1>). That means that in the past it was much smaller — as shown in **Figure 2**, part of **Practice 1**.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a1a7c478-9061-4160-ba43-301c081733e9",
                     "text": "©ESA",
                     "page": "The Universe Through Space-Time",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1af85131-52aa-49ee-8f9b-2409eb806900",
                     "text": "Hubble, Edwin",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1929",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 1: Compacting</b><br /><br /><i>Experiment with cotton</i><br /><br />1. Take a piece of cotton smaller than your hand.<br />2. Squeeze it tight in your hands.<br />What do you notice? The cotton becomes much smaller — just like the universe once was!<br /><br /><i>Experiment with the figure</i><br />1. Move the mouse or your finger over Figure 2.</div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"htCircle\" style=\"height: 40vh; width: 40vh; background-color: white; border:1px solid black; box-sizing: border-box;\" name=\"fig2\"></div></center><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 2</b>: The circle shrinks to show how the universe once contracted.</p><script>var divRadius = parseInt($(\".htCircle[name='fig2']\").width()); $(\".htCircle\").mouseenter(function(){ $(this).animate({ width: divRadius/4, height: divRadius/4 }, 'slow'); }).mouseleave(function(){ $(this).animate({ width: divRadius, height: divRadius }, 'slow'); });</script>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "In this figure, a circle shrinks when the mouse cursor or a finger hovers over it.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "But knowing that the universe was smaller does not tell us how it grew. That leads us to a reflection:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: How did it happen?</b><br /><br />How do you think the universe expanded over time — slowly and steadily, or very fast and sudden?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### The proposal\n\nOur idea of the universe’s size has changed a lot over the centuries. In <htdate0>, scientists estimated it at 300,000 light-years. A light-year is the distance light travels in one year. By <htdate1>, the estimate had grown to about 94 billion light-years (<htcite0>; <htcite1>).\n\nSize is not the only surprise. When we look in every direction, the universe looks almost the same everywhere — it is uniform (<htcite2>).\n\nThis beautiful picture, however, hides a puzzle that Alan H. Guth described and explained (<htcite3>; <htcite4>). Think about it:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "066ad64b-5a6b-4715-b2bd-b34a90164422",
                     "text": "NASA website",
                     "page": "NASA's Cosmic Time",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7cc05340-732f-4c3d-b260-5437670fbc99",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Shared Origin",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "015c0ffc-d894-404a-b795-eebc8aec2744",
                     "text": "Lincoln, Don",
                     "page": "Cosmic Inflation",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2015",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f33f6c12-670b-4c07-9549-5c1548bf4e12",
                     "text": "Guth, A. H.",
                     "page": "pp. 347",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1981",
                        "month": "1",
                        "day": "15"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "015c0ffc-d894-404a-b795-eebc8aec2744",
                     "text": "Lincoln, Don",
                     "page": "Cosmic Inflation",
