               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: Why are they the same?</b><br /><br />Imagine sending a signal at the same moment from two opposite ends of the universe, just as it began to expand. We would receive both signals long before either one could travel to the other end. In other words, those ends have never talked to each other.<br /><br />If, after 14 billion years, opposite ends still cannot exchange information, how can they look so alike?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Guth’s solution\n\nDoctor Alan Guth proposed a solution to this problem. At the beginning of the universe, when matter and energy were still unified, there was a monumental expansion in an extremely short time. The universe grew faster and faster, doubling again and again: 2, 4, 8, 16.\n\nSo even though the opposite ends have not been in contact for billions of years, there was a time when the universe was homogeneous — the same everywhere — and that sameness stayed after the giant expansion (<htcite0>; <htcite1>).\n\n#### Is there proof?\n\nScience is never only about theories: scientists test ideas and improve them all the time. The same is true for the Inflation Theory: Doctor Guth himself admitted this, and Doctor Lincoln explained it in his video (<htcite2>; <htcite3>).\n\nSome experiments suggest that inflation did in fact occur, while others show that we still need more evidence and more research.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f33f6c12-670b-4c07-9549-5c1548bf4e12",
                     "text": "Guth, A. H.",
                     "page": "pp. 350 - 354",
                     "date_time": {
